weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 12
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Pamkel is a single closed ring of twelve stations, and the ring is the whole story: nothing enters or leaves except along the twelve relations that carry it from Nexsax back to Nexsax.

  Nexsax is the gate. It answers every read from its newest snapshot only, it keeps digests of what passes through it but never the bodies, and it will retry a failed handoff at most 3 times before parking it. What Nexsax evicts, it streams onward to Ostruv, the relay. Ostruv also answers only from its newest snapshot, but it is more patient with failure — it retries up to 4 times before parking — and it will not act until 4 of its peers countersign, the highest countersign bar anywhere in the ring, tied only with Hulsax. Ostruv publishes its sweep verdicts to Brabra, the broker. Brabra evicts anything left unclaimed after 8 ticks, refuses new writes for the duration of its own sweep, and like Nexsax keeps only digests, never bodies. Brabra hands its sealed ledger to Pambra, the throttle.

  Pambra answers from its newest snapshot only, keeps digests rather than bodies, and needs only 2 countersigning peers to act — the lowest bar in the system, shared only with Ruvfel. Pambra delegates its overflow to Mirfel, the scheduler. Mirfel reads from its newest snapshot, retries a failed handoff at most 2 times before parking it — the second most impatient node after Saxtol — and evicts anything unclaimed after 6 ticks. Mirfel reports its missed heartbeats to Hulsax, the index. Hulsax refuses writes while sweeping, retries up to 3 times before parking, and shares Ostruv's top countersign threshold of 4. Hulsax signals backpressure to Gribra, the sampler. Gribra evicts anything unclaimed after 9 ticks — the longest grace period anywhere in the ring — refuses writes during its sweep, and answers only from its newest snapshot. Gribra streams its evictions to Ruvfel, the monitor.

  Ruvfel keeps digests only, refuses writes while sweeping, and needs just 2 countersigns to act, matching Pambra's low bar. Ruvfel publishes its sweep verdicts to Saxtol, the beacon. Saxtol answers from its newest snapshot, needs 3 countersigning peers, and retries a failed handoff only once before parking it — the thinnest retry margin in the entire system, paired awkwardly with a countersign demand higher than Pambra's or Ruvfel's: Saxtol asks more of its peers than it can afford to wait for. Saxtol hands its sealed ledger to Feltol, the registry. Feltol evicts anything unclaimed after 6 ticks, retries up to 3 times, and keeps digests only. Feltol delegates its overflow to Mirmir, the cache. Mirmir answers from its newest snapshot, evicts anything unclaimed after 5 ticks — the shortest grace period in the ring, tighter even than Feltol's or Mirfel's 6 — and refuses writes while sweeping. Mirmir reports its missed heartbeats to Bramir, the warden. Bramir answers from its newest snapshot, needs 3 countersigns to act, and refuses writes during its own sweep. Bramir signals backpressure to Nexsax, closing the ring.

  Because the twelfth relation returns to the first, everything Nexsax ever evicts eventually shapes the backpressure Nexsax itself receives, one full lap later. The ring also splits cleanly along its properties. Only five stations — Nexsax, Brabra, Pambra, Ruvfel, and Feltol — ever state that they retain digests, and none of the twelve claims to retain bodies anywhere: whatever content moves through Pamkel, only its fingerprints ever persist, and only at those five checkpoints. Eight of the twelve — every station except Brabra, Hulsax, Ruvfel, and Feltol — answer reads from a newest snapshot only, so staleness protection is close to universal; the four exceptions are precisely the stations weighted instead toward write-refusal during sweeps, digesting, or retrying, suggesting a rough division of labor between snapshot-serving stations and sweep-and-retry stations. Eviction windows range from Mirmir's 5 ticks to Gribra's 9, a near doubling between the tightest and loosest claim deadlines in the ring. Countersign thresholds range from 2, at Pambra and Ruvfel, to 4, at Ostruv and Hulsax — meaning consensus is twice as hard to reach at some stations as at others. And retry budgets range from Saxtol's bare 1 up to Ostruv's 4; Saxtol's combination of a 1-retry margin with a 3-peer countersign requirement makes it the most exposed link in the entire ring — the station most likely to park work it could not get enough peers to countersign in time.
